  "summary": "A British colonial architectural style combining Indo-Islamic elements with Victorian Gothic revival.",
  "description": "Characterized by onion domes, minarets, jaali (perforated stone screens), and chhatri (canopies) combined with European Gothic arches, pointed windows, and classical columns. It was developed by British architects as an imperial style to visually represent British authority while incorporating recognizable Indian elements.",
  "culturalContext": "Indo-Saracenic was the British Raj's official hybrid: engineered by colonial architects such as Robert Fellowes Chisholm and Vincent Esch for Bombay, Madras and princely states from the 1870s, it married Mughal domes, chhatri kiosks and cusped arches to Victorian steel structure and Gothic massing. Princes commissioned it to broadcast modernity with native legitimacy — Bombay's Prince of Wales Museum and Mysore Palace remain textbook cases — and post-independence India kept many as civic landmarks.",
